  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Support to IFRC Ghana Urban Water Sanitation and Hygiene cluster</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ghana Red Cross and Ghanese households</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ghana</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Partnerships between national societies and local water and sanitation utilities are rare. Utilities are not only sidetracked during normal times, but also during emergency WASH responses utilities are often not well involved, which can lead to utilities being harmed and by emergency WASH responses. Pre-crisis Implementing partnerships between national societies and local water and sanitation utilities lead to a better inclusion during times of emergencies.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Netherlands Red Cross (NLRC) recognizes the crucial role of public water and sanitation service providers in supplying sustainable water and sanitation services in urban context and specifically for the urban-poor. In addition, the NLRC sees the RCRC movement as a strong complimentary organization to public water and sanitation service providers, due to our volunteer based and our expertise in community involvement which is an expertise often lacking at the more technocratic public service providers. NLRC can specifically support service providers in the field of emergency preparedness and response, community engagement and accountability.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">With the urban population showing steady growth over the past decade, the Government of Ghana is faced with the challenge of coping with the growing demand for Water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) services, especially in deprived urban settlements. Currently, around 70% of the nation&apos;s urban population lives in congested, sub-standard structures with little incentive to invest in a more permanent solution. Most parts of the peri-urban areas fall outside the formal planning framework of the State authorities, and therefore lack legal standing in terms of utility services. As a result, the vast majority of the people without access to water and sanitation services are found in the poorer communities and delivering safe, sustainable and affordable water and sanitation services in poor urban communities remains a major challenge.

Through the localization program, NLRC is able to provide support to an urban WASH pilot in Ghana. Within the pilot, Vitens Evides International (VEI) and Ghana Water Company Limited (GWCL partner with IFRC Ghana and NLRC, aiming at improving sustainable access to adequate water services for the urban population in the Ashanti region. In addition, through this collaboration, we also aim to build a strong partnership between the local water utility and the national society. This by making use of project-activities to materialize this partnership (and to positively contribute to resilient communities), and to build a collaboration package that can be copied in any country where our national society is interested. Overall the pilot will be contributing to objective 2, 4 and 5 of the Localization program.</narrative>
  </description>
